Thanks for the report! I found two likely causes.

**Cause 1:** Pro mode sets `caseSensitive = true`, so a sentence typed with different capitalization (for example "the Edo period..." instead of "The Edo period...") is marked NG even when the words are right.

**Cause 2:** `isCorrect` only trims the ends of the input. Repeated spaces inside the sentence make the comparison fail.

**Fix:** Normalize whitespace on both sides before comparing, and make Pro case-insensitive:

```js
function isCorrect(value, answer) {
  const clean = value.trim().replace(/\s+/g, " ");
  const target = answer.trim().replace(/\s+/g, " ");
  return caseSensitive
    ? clean === target
    : clean.toLowerCase() === target.toLowerCase();
}
```

and in `startLevel`, change the Pro branch to `caseSensitive = false;`.

With this change, correctly typed Pro sentences are accepted. I'll update the PR with this fix.
